WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:08.099 --> 00:00:13.139
In today’s video, we help you further understand
some of the important FDA rules around labeling

00:00:13.139 --> 00:00:14.450
your product.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:19.539
Hi, I’m Michael H. Cohen, founding attorney
of the Cohen Healthcare Law Group.

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:24.279
We help healthcare industry clients just like
you navigate healthcare and FDA legal and

00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:30.050
regulatory issues every day, so you can continue
to scale, or launch your health and wellness

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.050
product.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:36.710
In recent video, we talked about how FDA looks
not just at the label that you peel on your

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:42.710
product, but everything, everything you say
in your marketing about your product. FDA

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:46.460
calls all of this your labeling— not just
one label.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:51.379
What FDA is looking for is whether you are
sticking to structure/function claims, which

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:56.229
you’re allowed to make for supplements if
you dealing with dietary supplement, or, whether

00:00:56.229 --> 00:01:00.039
you are making disease claims which we also
call them drug claims, which are unlawful

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:05.590
and will cause your dietary supplement or
cosmetic product to be regulated as a drug.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:10.060
As the manufacturer or distributor of a dietary
supplement, you can’t afford to have your

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:15.000
product regulated as a drug, because you don’t
want to spend years and years and millions

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.630
of dollars on clinical trials establishing
safety and efficacy.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:24.750
You simply want to say, “supports” this
or “boost” that, and the overall context

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.620
have to be a safe claim, get the product on
the shelves and get it sold.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.860
But, sometimes it helps your marketing – it
boosts your marketing, it supports your marketing

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.570
– to be a little more aggressive in your
claims.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:40.540
That in itself is not a bad thing, but that’s
where it can be worth a lot of money to have

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:45.409
a lawyer who is proactive, who understands
the nuances, who knows where you can push

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:48.189
the marketing, probably.

00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:53.280
And when you can be assertive but when your
claims are so aggressive that they’re likely

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.350
going to tip the balance into drug claim territory.
And I say probably, because there’s always

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:00.549
a judgment call, and you always make a business
judgment.

00:02:00.549 --> 00:02:04.140
Now, large companies, you can’t make a move
without going to the lawyers, you cannot put

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.090
a claim on the website, without the legal
department coming down, a lot of times coming

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:12.810
down heavy, because if you get it wrong, the
company gets slammed by FDA and, you’ll

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.220
also have headline news. So you don’t want
to be on the news.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.940
Smaller companies sometimes don’t realize
this, and they typically tip-toe around trying

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.200
to get the cheapest quote for a label review.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:29.920
Sure, you can train a paralegal to review
a label for technical compliance, but, that’s

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.940
not the same as having good sense and experience
and judgment as to the nuances of product

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:41.110
labeling (including the claim) and understanding
FDA’s tolerance—or not—for a little

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:42.540
marketing flair.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.000
The difference between a claim that draws
FDA fire and one that does not can be very

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.010
subtle; and, so can effective marketing campaign
– a good campaign vs. one that is dull,

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.530
dry, plain vanilla boring, just because the
lawyer always says no.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.840
Please note, the phrase “plain vanilla”
has not been approved by FDA. I’m just kidding,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.200
I’m giving you a metaphor.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:09.341
FDA doesn’t have to approve everything,
certainly not plain vanilla; but, they can

00:03:09.341 --> 00:03:13.510
do something very simple: go into the search
bar of Google and they look for terms and

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:18.280
in this way FDA can very easily catch you
if your marketing terms make claims that FDA

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.240
considers unlawful for dietary supplements
or cosmetics.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.860
One last tip, don’t rely on other companies
to do the work for you, even if they are big

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.860
manufacturers or redistributors and assure
you that all is well and done well. Don’t

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.530
rely on them vetting the claims, get your
own legal counsel. I used to say “Don’t

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.040
roll the dice, get legal advice.”

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:41.010
Thanks for watching. If you still have questions,
